  "raw": "ima Vavi\nIn its draft manifesto, Cosatu has called for the \"expropriation of land without compensation, targeted delivering of support to the people and recovery of some losses realised by the state\" among other nationalist measures.\nCosatu president Sizwe Pamla is currently criticised by sections inside and outside his sprawling alliance for marginalising party deputy secretary-general Irvin Jim.\nA former head of Unisa's economics department whose there been daggers in each others' hands, Pamla was elected on a ticket that included Jim who took Oupa Magashule's name out as an option winning 56% of the vote.\nPamla rode into power at a time when Cosatu affiliates federations were weighing how united they really are as their ideological differences were tearing them apart. One group backs radical populism while another tries to hold onto unionism within the context of a mixed economy.\nComplicating things further was Pamla's controversial decision to make Real India chief",
  "serialized": "ima Vavi In its draft manifesto, Cosatu has called for the \"expropriation of land without compensation, targeted delivering of support to the people and recovery of some losses realised by the state\" among other nationalist measures. Cosatu president Sizwe Pamla is currently criticised by sections inside and outside his sprawling alliance for marginalising party deputy secretary-general Irvin Jim. A former head of Unisa's economics department whose there been daggers in each others' hands, Pamla was elected on a ticket that included Jim who took Oupa Magashule's name out as an option winning 56% of the vote. Pamla rode into power at a time when Cosatu affiliates federations were weighing how united they really are as their ideological differences were tearing them apart. One group backs radical populism while another tries to hold onto unionism within the context of a mixed economy. Complicating things further was Pamla's controversial decision to make Real India chief",
